WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.550
Google “death” and this is what you’ll
get as one of the first images.

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:13.070
The figure is so ingrained in our culture
it's hard to separate death from, well Death.

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:18.000
This iconic image of death personified is
a skeleton that wields a scythe, and is named

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.680
the “Grim” Reaper.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.170
But why?

00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:25.880
To answer these questions we have to look
at ancient religion and artwork, Aramaic scripture,

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:27.990
and yes, the Black Plague.

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:32.680
How we conceptualize death, and how we choose
to personify it changes over time.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:37.660
And there’s more to the Grim Reaper than
just doom and gloom.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:40.760
[Monstrum intro.]

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:46.500
Macabre images of Death personified have been
around since at least the late 13th Century.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:50.890
This illustration, “The Legend of the Three
Living and Three Dead,” is often considered

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:55.680
to be the first known European image of an
animated, skeletal death figure.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:00.430
It’s based on a popular story from the second
half of the thirteenth century.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:06.630
And I mean a very popular story, with versions
in French, English, Italian, and German.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:11.800
The story goes that three finely dressed young
men, aristocrats or sometimes kings, are out

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:17.860
hunting when they come across three animated
corpses all in varying stages of decay.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:24.070
To their horror, they recognize them as their
own ancestors, or perhaps even the men themselves.

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:29.510
The corpses warn the living trio of the fleetingness
of life, the grossness of death, and the need

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:33.680
for repentance – turning the men toward
a more righteous path.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:38.340
The frescoes of the Camposanto in Pisa, a
part of the Piazza del Duomo known as the

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:43.150
“monumental cemetery” or “holy field”
were also inspired by this story.

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:47.490
The frescos portray a flying “Death” with
bat wings holding a scythe.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:53.270
That same century an event of global proportions
fundamentally changed humanity’s relationship

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:58.670
with and perception of death—the bubonic
plague, or what we now commonly call the Black

00:01:58.670 --> 00:01:59.670
Death.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:05.119
Its monumental spread in the mid-14th century
in Europe, Asia, and Africa killed a huge

00:02:05.119 --> 00:02:07.750
portion of the global population.

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:11.989
How did we even attempt to manage the horror
of such widespread death?

00:02:11.989 --> 00:02:16.549
To give us more insight into the Black Death,
here’s Caitlin Doughty of Ask a Mortician.

00:02:16.549 --> 00:02:19.169
So Caitlin, what do we know about how people
processed death during the bubonic plague?

00:02:19.169 --> 00:02:20.169
Hi Emily, hi everyone.

00:02:20.169 --> 00:02:25.701
So, let’s understand that by the end of
the 14th century, anywhere from 20%, which

00:02:25.701 --> 00:02:32.489
is already a huge percentage, to 75% of the
global population had succumbed to the bubonic

00:02:32.489 --> 00:02:33.489
plague.

00:02:33.489 --> 00:02:38.209
And yes that’s quite a range but there was
inconsistent, inaccurate record keeping and

00:02:38.209 --> 00:02:40.430
no reliable census or data collection.

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:47.739
But, any way you slice it— just a monumental
amount of death, causing economic, psychosocial,

00:02:47.739 --> 00:02:51.219
and cultural effects on the population.

00:02:51.219 --> 00:02:56.609
Turning Death into a humanoid figure— Death,
why he’s just like us!— may have been

00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:58.389
a way to help us cope.

00:02:58.389 --> 00:03:04.419
As the plague continued to rage on, skeletons
and even half decomposed corpses, became a

00:03:04.419 --> 00:03:06.390
common visual trope.

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:11.390
They emphasized the universality of death
and served as a reminder of death.

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:15.419
“As I am so you too will be, folks!”

00:03:15.419 --> 00:03:20.569
“The Three Living and the Three Dead”
story, which I love, was just the beginning.

00:03:20.569 --> 00:03:26.859
Churches and cemeteries across Europe featured
allegorical scenes of the Danse Macabre—showing

00:03:26.859 --> 00:03:32.760
skeletons, corpses, and living people from
all backgrounds, all walks of life interacting

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:33.870
together.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:38.030
King or pauper, no one was safe from the plague.

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:41.169
Money and status can’t save you!

00:03:41.169 --> 00:03:46.120
We also see the emergence of a singular Death
standing over bodies or collecting the dead,

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:52.059
“Triumph of Death” motifs, that illustrate
the huge numbers of dead the grave could consume.

00:03:52.059 --> 00:03:56.829
This period also marks the appearance of artistic
renderings of these skeletons with weapons—a

00:03:56.829 --> 00:04:00.309
bow, sword, sickle, or scythe.

00:04:00.309 --> 00:04:04.959
For example, in the mid 14th century, the
poet Petrarch, wrote his poem Triumphus Mortis:

00:04:04.959 --> 00:04:09.659
Trionfo della Morte (Triumph of Death) after
viewing the Camposanto frescos.

00:04:09.659 --> 00:04:12.559
The text’s popularity [Petrarch’s Triumph
of death] inspired more illustrations and

00:04:12.559 --> 00:04:17.620
paintings depicting Death as a skeleton or
a corpse standing on a cart riding over a

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:21.390
dying population, with a large scythe over
her shoulder.

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:23.580
That’s right, her shoulder.

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:29.050
Death’s gender can be either male or female
depending on the country and time period...

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:33.790
Those of us in the Slavic and Germanic-language
groups more often imagine a male Grim Reaper,

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:38.390
while a female version has been more dominant
in Romance and Latinate languages.

00:04:38.390 --> 00:04:43.360
Don’t forget that much of European culture
harkens back to the same Greco-Roman roots.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:48.430
Thus, male “death” gods in Greek and Roman
culture deeply influenced the Grim Reaper’s

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:49.430
appearance.

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:53.680
Thanatos [θænətɒs] the winged Greek
personification of nonviolent death, has been

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:58.750
compared to the Grim Reaper but there are
two main differences—Thanatos is always

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:01.200
male and he carries away the dead.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:05.660
The Grim Reaper is more often conceptualized
as forcefully claiming the deceased, cutting

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:06.970
down lives indiscriminately.

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:10.840
There’s [Chronos/Χρόνος], Heronos
the male Greek god of time.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:14.620
Who is often confused with Kronos [ˈkroʊnɒs
or Κρόνος] the ancient Greek god connected

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:19.680
to agriculture portrayed as an old man with
a beard, sickle in hand.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:24.500
The physical resemblance of these three gods
and the natural associations with harvest,

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:29.200
the passing of seasons, and time aligned them
with death and each other.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:33.650
When Renaissance artists and authors were
looking for ways to depict death, they adopted

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:40.270
elements from Thanatos, Heronos, and Kronos—and
replaced the hand-held sickle with the larger,

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:42.590
more efficient scythe.

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:45.530
Just butting in here for some scythe facts.

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:50.420
During this same time in the early 14th century
when we see this increase in imagery of Death

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:56.840
as a skeleton in art and literature, the long-handled
scythe that you can use standing up, was gaining

00:05:56.840 --> 00:06:00.860
popularity in the corn harvests of medieval
Europe.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:02.980
Scythes went viral in the corn harvesting
scene.

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:09.080
It was a faster way of mowing than the sickle,
the increased productivity meant fewer laborers

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:10.080
were needed.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:15.220
But because of the strength and height needed
to use this larger tool, it was perceived

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:17.400
as a masculine object.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:21.400
Those who wielded sickles in the fields were
called reapers.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:24.370
Those who used scythes were “mowers.”

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:29.450
So, the Grim Reaper should technically be
the Grim Mower.

00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:34.020
Which just doesn’t have the same ring to
it.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:39.270
Up to this point in Medieval history, there
are clear associations of Death with time

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:44.880
and the harvest which helps explain why this
Grim Reaper, this figure representing Death,

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:51.550
is portrayed with scythe in hand “mowing
down” the living quickly and in great numbers—

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:54.600
much like the Black Plague.

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:58.540
Personifications of Death continued to be
popular reminding the living of the inevitability

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:00.330
of their end.

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:06.290
But Death was also a symbol of compassion—waiting
with open arms in the afterlife, a sign of

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:08.760
comfort and acceptance.

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:13.550
From this came association of Death with love,
leading to its portrayal as a bridegroom of

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:16.410
young Maidens during the Renaissance period.

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:21.060
And eventually, as the eroticized “demon
lover” which gained popularity in the 18th

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:22.860
and 19th centuries.

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:27.090
So how did Death come to be named the Grim
Reaper?

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:30.900
That actually didn’t happen until hundreds
of years after the Black Death.

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:35.150
The word “grim” has been in use since
the late 10th century to mean something “fierce,

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:40.750
cruel, savage or harsh in disposition or action;”
or “fiercely angry.”

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:45.910
The moniker “The Grim Reaper'' in reference
to the personification of Death first appears

00:07:45.910 --> 00:07:52.210
in English literature in 1847 in The Circle
of Human Life, a partial translation of an

00:07:52.210 --> 00:07:56.650
earlier German devotional text (Stunden Christlicher
Andacht) from 1841 that tracks the life of

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:58.150
a Christian.

00:07:58.150 --> 00:07:59.790
So there you have it.

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:04.451
Death as a figure that takes lives with a
scythe has been around for a long time, but

00:08:04.451 --> 00:08:06.840
the name Grim Reaper is more modern.

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:07.840
And since the 19th century, it's stuck.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:11.030
The Grim Reaper pops up everywhere in popular
culture.

00:08:11.030 --> 00:08:16.941
The interpretation can be more lighthearted
like in Death Takes a Holiday from 1934, Meet

00:08:16.941 --> 00:08:21.340
Joe Black from 1998, or the TV show Dead Like
Me.

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:27.340
The Reaper here is meant to humanize Death
and show that they have motivations and physical

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:28.700
forms much like humans.

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:29.700
[opening credits Dead Like Me].

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:35.169
And it makes sense that in times of mass death,
we know a little something about that right

00:08:35.169 --> 00:08:42.089
now, it helps to see Death as not some terrifying,
invisible, indiscriminate force bearing down

00:08:42.089 --> 00:08:47.649
on us, but something with a face, maybe even
a personality.

00:08:47.649 --> 00:08:53.769
Better that Death is like a bureaucratically
focused DMV worker, annoying but relatable,

00:08:53.769 --> 00:08:56.050
than a black hole of nothingness.

00:08:56.050 --> 00:09:01.139
It’s easy to see why that would make us
less anxious, and I’m fascinated to see

00:09:01.139 --> 00:09:07.290
how art and literature personify death as
we continue to fight, and hopefully recover

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:10.160
from, the pandemic.

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:15.620
Like Caitlin said, more relatable interpretations
of the Grim Reaper in the 20th and 21st centuries

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:22.069
lessened the blow of his scythe so to speak
[Bill and Ted’s Excellent adventure clip],

00:09:22.069 --> 00:09:27.149
but whenever there is a large loss of life
in the real world, Death returns to its allegorical

00:09:27.149 --> 00:09:29.529
role as something to be feared.

00:09:29.529 --> 00:09:35.220
Like in 1987, when Australia used the cloaked
figure to raise awareness about AIDS.

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:41.120
Or more recently in 2020, when political cartoonists
(and one Florida lawyer) used the Grim Reaper

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:44.120
to emphasize the dangers of COVID-19.

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:48.860
Around the world people evoked the dramatic
figure to emphasize high death tolls by the

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:54.170
global pandemic or as a warning of what might
meet someone who fails to recognize the significant

00:09:54.170 --> 00:09:56.050
dangers of the virus.

00:09:56.050 --> 00:09:59.149
Death is both personal and collective.

00:09:59.149 --> 00:10:02.029
After all, Death is universal.

